c48db623-59a9-4652-bebe-ae3ed12c4e42	0e2c91e4-d620-473f-a3bc-205bc76fc4ed	1	Module 2	\N	This is module 2	\N	2026-09-01 16:14:57.054686+03
1f5e72ef-c2d1-44e0-91f1-81ac14e3716d	0e2c91e4-d620-473f-a3bc-205bc76fc4ed	3	Module 2	\N	This is module 2	\N	2026-09-01 16:15:01.678832+03
b92d1d21-f5a7-4908-8fec-42212dae5d88	e8306116-17e7-4f87-8d9d-8a4c724f58c1	1	Module 2 — Occupational Health and Toxicology	Recognising workplace hazards, understanding exposure, and selecting effective controls.	Learning objectives\nBy the end of this note, you should be able to:\n• distinguish physical, chemical, biological, ergonomic, and psychosocial hazards;\n• explain the main routes through which toxic substances enter the body;\n• apply the hierarchy of controls to a workplace risk; and\n• differentiate acute effects from chronic effects.\n\n1. Occupational health\nOccupational health protects the physical, mental, and social wellbeing of people at work. It focuses on preventing work-related injury and disease—not merely treating them after they occur.\n\n2. Common workplace hazards\nPhysical hazards include noise, heat, vibration, radiation, and unsafe machinery. Chemical hazards include gases, vapours, dusts, fumes, and liquids. Biological hazards include bacteria, viruses, fungi, and contaminated material. Ergonomic hazards arise from poor posture, repetition, lifting, or badly designed workstations. Psychosocial hazards include excessive workload, harassment, fatigue, and low control over work.\n\n3. Routes of exposure\nA harmful substance may enter the body through inhalation, skin or eye absorption, ingestion, or injection through a puncture wound. In many workplaces, inhalation is the most significant route because airborne contaminants can reach deep into the lungs.\n\n4. Dose and health effects\nToxicity describes a substance’s ability to cause harm. Risk depends on both the hazard and the exposure. Acute effects appear soon after a short or high exposure; chronic effects develop after repeated or long-term exposure. The dose, duration, frequency, route of entry, and individual susceptibility all influence the outcome.\n\n5. Hierarchy of controls\nUse controls in this order:\n1. Elimination — remove the hazard completely.\n2. Substitution — replace it with something less hazardous.\n3. Engineering controls — isolate people using ventilation, guards, or enclosure.\n4. Administrative controls — change procedures, schedules, training, or access.\n5. Personal protective equipment — use respirators, gloves, goggles, or protective clothing.\nPPE is the final layer, not the first solution, because its effectiveness depends heavily on correct selection and consistent use.\n\nWorked example\nA laboratory uses a volatile solvent in an open container. Giving every worker a mask is weaker than replacing the solvent, enclosing the process, or installing local exhaust ventilation. The best response starts at the top of the hierarchy and combines controls when necessary.\n\nQuick self-check\n1. Why are hazard and risk not the same thing?\n2. Give one example of each major hazard category.\n3. What is the difference between an acute and a chronic health effect?\n4. Why is PPE placed last in the hierarchy of controls?\n\nKey takeaway\nEffective occupational health practice identifies the hazard, evaluates the actual exposure, and controls the risk as close to its source as possible.	10f7cfa7-8c69-47f3-9f6a-f769f89240d3	2026-09-01 16:29:10.650944+03
75e76049-342c-44db-abb4-93ee7d2841c5	e8306116-17e7-4f87-8d9d-8a4c724f58c1	2	Module 2 — Occupational Health and Toxicology	Recognising workplace hazards, understanding exposure, and selecting effective controls.	## Learning objectives\n\nBy the end of this note, you should be able to:\n\n- distinguish **physical, chemical, biological, ergonomic, and psychosocial hazards**;\n- explain the main routes through which toxic substances enter the body;\n- apply the hierarchy of controls to a workplace risk; and\n- differentiate *acute effects* from *chronic effects*.\n\n> **Core idea:** Occupational health prevents work-related injury and disease—it does not merely treat harm after it occurs.\n\n## 1. Occupational health\n\nOccupational health protects the physical, mental, and social wellbeing of people at work. Good practice identifies hazards early, evaluates real exposure, and controls risk as close to its source as possible.\n\n### Common workplace hazards\n\n| Hazard category | Typical examples |\n| --- | --- |\n| Physical | Noise, heat, vibration, radiation, unsafe machinery |\n| Chemical | Gases, vapours, dusts, fumes, liquids |\n| Biological | Bacteria, viruses, fungi, contaminated material |\n| Ergonomic | Poor posture, repetitive work, lifting, badly designed workstations |\n| Psychosocial | Excessive workload, harassment, fatigue, low control over work |\n\n## 2. Routes of exposure\n\nA harmful substance may enter the body through:\n\n1. **Inhalation** — breathing gases, vapours, fumes, or dust.\n2. **Absorption** — entry through the skin or eyes.\n3. **Ingestion** — swallowing contaminated food, drink, or material.\n4. **Injection** — entry through a puncture wound or sharp object.\n\nIn many workplaces, inhalation is especially important because airborne contaminants can reach deep into the lungs.\n\n## 3. Dose and health effects\n\n*Toxicity* describes a substance’s ability to cause harm. **Risk** depends on both the hazard and the level of exposure.\n\n- **Acute effects** appear soon after a short or high exposure.\n- **Chronic effects** develop after repeated or long-term exposure.\n\nThe dose, duration, frequency, route of entry, and individual susceptibility all influence the outcome.\n\n## 4. Hierarchy of controls\n\nUse controls in this order:\n\n1. **Elimination** — remove the hazard completely.\n2. **Substitution** — replace it with something less hazardous.\n3. **Engineering controls** — isolate people using ventilation, guards, or enclosure.\n4. **Administrative controls** — change procedures, schedules, training, or access.\n5. **Personal protective equipment (PPE)** — use respirators, gloves, goggles, or protective clothing.\n\n++Control at the source++ is usually more reliable than depending on individual behaviour. **PPE is the final layer, not the first solution.**\n\n## Worked example\n\nA laboratory uses a volatile solvent in an open container. Giving every worker a mask is weaker than replacing the solvent, enclosing the process, or installing local exhaust ventilation. The best response starts at the top of the hierarchy and combines controls when necessary.\n\n## Quick self-check\n\n- [ ] Why are *hazard* and *risk* not the same thing?\n- [ ] Give one example of each major hazard category.\n- [ ] What is the difference between an acute and a chronic health effect?\n- [ ] Why is PPE placed last in the hierarchy of controls?\n\n---\n\n### Key takeaway\n\n**Identify the hazard, evaluate the exposure, and control the risk as close to its source as possible.**	10f7cfa7-8c69-47f3-9f6a-f769f89240d3	2026-09-01 16:42:33.244511+03
\.


