use harm. **Risk** depends on both the hazard and the level of exposure.\n\n- **Acute effects** appear soon after a short or high exposure.\n- **Chronic effects** develop after repeated or long-term exposure.\n\nThe dose, duration, frequency, route of entry, and individual susceptibility all influence the outcome.\n\n## 4. Hierarchy of controls\n\nUse controls in this order:\n\n1. **Elimination** — remove the hazard completely.\n2. **Substitution** — replace it with something less hazardous.\n3. **Engineering controls** — isolate people using ventilation, guards, or enclosure.\n4. **Administrative controls** — change procedures, schedules, training, or access.\n5. **Personal protective equipment (PPE)** — use respirators, gloves, goggles, or protective clothing.\n\n++Control at the source++ is usually more reliable than depending on individual behaviour. **PPE is the final layer, not the first solution.**\n\n## Worked example\n\nA laboratory uses a volatile solvent in an open container. Giving every worker a mask is weaker than replacing the solvent, enclosing the process, or installing local exhaust ventilation. The best response starts at the top of the hierarchy and combines controls when necessary.\n\n## Quick self-check\n\n- [ ] Why are *hazard* and *risk* not the same thing?\n- [ ] Give one example of each major hazard category.\n- [ ] What is the difference between an acute and a chronic health effect?\n- [ ] Why is PPE placed last in the hierarchy of controls?\n\n---\n\n### Key takeaway\n\n**Identify the hazard, evaluate the exposure, and control the risk as close to its source as possible.**	ehs101-module-2-occupational-health	2	published	2026-09-01 16:29:10.650944+03	[]	10f7cfa7-8c69-47f3-9f6a-f769f89240d3	2	af3a3520-d8e5-47f5-b361-35344af523a1	af3a3520-d8e5-47f5-b361-35344af523a1	2026-09-01 16:29:10.650944+03	2026-09-01 16:42:33.244511+03
