 {"id": "topic-4-6", "title": "Pages 4–6", "excerpt": "3. Semi - Supervised Learning Semi - Supervised learning is a type of Machine Learning algorithm that lies between Supervised and Unsupervised machine learning . It represents the i ntermediate ground between Supervised (With Labelled training data) and Unsupervised learning (with no labelled training data) algorithms and uses the combination of labelled and unlabeled datasets during the training period. A lthough Semi - supervised learn ing is the middle ground between supervised and unsupervised learning and operates on the data that consists of a few labels, it mostly consists of unlabeled data. As labels are costly, but for corporate purposes, they may have few labels. It is completely different from supervised and unsupervised learning as they are based on the presence & absence of labels. To overcome the drawbacks of supervised learning and unsupervised learning algorithms, the concept of Semi - supervised learning is introduced . The main aim of semi - supervised learning is to effectively use all the available data, rather than only labelled data like in supervised learning. Initially, similar data is cl ustered along with an unsupervised learning algorithm, and further, it", "pageEnd": 6, "summary": "3. Semi - Supervised Learning Semi - Supervised learning is a type of Machine Learning algorithm that lies between Supervised and Unsupervised machine learning .", "keyTerms": ["learning", "data", "supervised", "unsupervised", "actions", "sequence"], "pageStart": 4, "likelyDoubts": ["How does Pages 4–6 fit into the rest of the document?"], "estimatedMinutes": 11}, {"id": "topic-7-9", "title": "Pages 7–9", "excerpt": "In linear regression(univariate regression) the aim is to draw a best fit line through the data that captures relation between independent and dependent variables with least residual error. The above figure shows an example for binary classification. The purpose of the classifier(line) is to classify the data into two classes - class A and class B. S UPERVISED L EARNING Q) explain the concept o f hyposthesis Q) Explain Ockham’s razor concept The task of supervised learning is this: Given a training set of N example input – output pairs ( x 1 ,y 1 ) , ( x 2 ,y 2 ) ,. .. ( x N ,y N ) , where each y j was generated by an unknown function y = f ( x ) , discover a function h that approximates the true function f . Here x and y can be any value; they need not be numbers. The function h is a hypothesis . Learning is a search through the space of possible hypotheses for one that will perform well, even on new examples beyond the training set. To measure the accuracy of a hypothesis we give it a test set of examples that are distinct from the training set. The hypothesis generalizes well if it correctly predicts the value of y for novel examples. When the output y is one of a finite set of ", "pageEnd": 9, "summary": "In linear regression(univariate regression) the aim is to draw a best fit line through the data that captures relation between independent and dependent variables with least residual error.", "keyTerms": ["data", "example", "training", "hypothesis", "fitting", "polynomial"], "pageStart": 7, "likelyDoubts": ["When the output y is one of a finite set of values (such as sunny, cloudy or rainy ), the learning problem is called classification , and is called Boolean or binary classificati on if there are only two values.", "When y is a number (such as tomorrow’s temperature), the learning problem is called regression .", "When the output y is one of a finite set of values (such as sunny, cloudy or rainy ), the learning problem is called classification , and is called Boolean or binary classification CLASSIFICATION if there are only two values."], "estimatedMinutes": 19}, {"id": "topic-10-12", "title": "Pages 10–12", "excerpt": "Q) explain Linear regr ession /Univariate regression Q) explain gradient de scent method for finding best fit line in linear regression Q) types of gradient descent methods Linear regression (Univariate regression) using gradient descent A univariate linear function (a straight line) with input x and output y has the form y = w 1 x + w 0 , where w 0 and w 1 are real - valued coefficients to be learne d. Example Above Figure shows an example of a training set of n points in the x, y plane, each point representing the size in square feet and the price of a house offered for sale. The task of finding the h w that best fits these data is called linear regression . To fit a line to the data, all we have to do is find the values of the weights [ w 0 , w 1 ] that minimize the empirical loss (error) . 718 Chapter 18. Learning from Examples 300 400 500 600 700 800 900 1000 500 1000 1500 2000 2500 3000 3500 House price in $1000 House size in square feet w 0 w 1 Loss (a) (b) Figure 18.13 (a) Data points of price versus floor space of houses for sale in Berkeley, CA, in July 2009, along with the linear function hypothesis that minimizes squared error loss: y = 0 . 232 x + 246 . (b) Plot of the", "pageEnd": 12, "summary": "Q) explain Linear regr ession /Univariate regression Q) explain gradient de scent method for finding best fit line in linear regression Q) types of gradient descent methods Linear regression (Univariate regression) using gradient descent A univariate linear function (a straight line) with input x and output y has the form y = w 1 x + w 0 , where w 0 and w 1 ", "keyTerms": ["loss", "linear", "function", "regression", "gradient", "descent"], "pageStart": 10, "likelyDoubts": ["How does Pages 10–12 fit into the rest of the document?"], "estimatedMinutes": 20}, {"id": "topic-13-15", "title": "Pages 13–15", "excerpt": "Q) explain various types of regression Types of Regression • Univariate ( linear and polynomial) • M ultivariate or multiple regression Prepared By Abin Philip, Asst Prof, Toc H. Reference: Introduction to Machine Learning, II edition, Ethem Alpaydin 21 The MLE that we find by taking the partial derivatives of the log likelihood and setting them equal to 0 are 3.10 Regression A regression problem is the problem of determining a relation between one or more independent variables and an output variable which is a real continuous variable, given a set of observed values of the set of independent variables and the corres ponding values of the output variable. 2 2 σ 2 The MLE that we fi nd by taking the p artial d e rivatives o f the log l ikeli- ho o d and setting them equal to 0 are m = t x t N (4.8) s 2 = t ( x t − m ) 2 N L ( μ , σ | X ) = − 2 lo g ( 2 π ) − N lo g σ − t 2 σ 2 The MLE that we fi nd by taking the p artial d e rivatives o f the log l ikeli- ho o d and setting them equal to 0 are m = t x t N (4.8) s 2 = t ( x t − m ) 2 N APJKTU, CS 476 Introduction to Machine Learning , Module 3 Polynomial regression Q) Explai n Logistic Regression with example Linear classification wi", "pageEnd": 15, "summary": "Q) explain various types of regression Types of Regression • Univariate ( linear and polynomial) • M ultivariate or multiple regression Prepared By Abin Philip, Asst Prof, Toc H.", "keyTerms": ["regression", "logistic", "linear", "used", "learning", "variable"], "pageStart": 13, "likelyDoubts": ["How does Pages 13–15 fit into the rest of the document?"], "estimatedMinutes": 8}, {"id": "topic-16-18", "title": "Pages 16–18", "excerpt": "o The sigmoid function is a mathemat ical function used to map the predicted values to probabilities. o It maps any real value into another value within a range of 0 and 1. o The value of the logistic regression must be between 0 and 1, which cannot go beyond this limit, so it forms a curve like the \\"S\\" form. The S - form curve is called the Sigmoid function or the logistic function. o In logistic regression, we use the concept of the threshold value, which defines the probability of either 0 or 1. Such as values above the threshold value tends to 1, and a value below the threshold values tends to 