l discuss: Conditions that required use of fall protection Options available to protect workers Slide 5 Fall Protection Falls are the leading cause", "topicId": "topic-1-10", "keyTerms": ["fall", "slide", "protection", "construction", "must", "safety"], "priority": "understand", "likelyDoubts": ["When fall protection is needed?"], "estimatedMinutes": 5}, "targetConcepts": ["MODULE 3"], "scopeBoundaries": ["Ground every document claim in extracted pages the student can access.", "Cite exact page numbers and do not reconstruct missing or unextracted content."], "knownFailureDetails": [], "learningPathPosition": null}	{"door": "uploaded_document", "label": "Construction Safety, Tunnelling & Ergonomics — Module 3 · selected pages", "intentId": "755ff7a0-18ff-4a38-b614-8a96813d809d", "citations": [{"page": 1, "label": "Construction Safety, Tunnelling & Ergonomics — Module 3 (p. 1–10)", "excerpt": "Slide 1 MODULE 3 Slide 2 Major hazards of construction Falls Electrocution Being struck by falling objects Trapped during excavation Slide 3 Major hazards of construction 1 Slide 4 Fall Protection This section will discuss: Conditions that required use of fall protection Options available to protect workers Slide 5 Fall Protection Falls are the leading cause of fatalities in the construction industry Conditions that required use of fall protection A fall from as little as 4-6 feet Can cause loss of work In some cases death Slide 6 When fall protection is needed? Walkways & ramps Open sides & edges Holes Concrete forms & rebar Excavations Roofs Wall openings Bricklaying Residential Construction Slide 7 Safety Nets Hand Rails Safety Harness (PFAS) Equipment guards Fall protection systems must be in place before work start Fall protection and prevention options Slide 8 Must be properly trained Key requirements No free fall more than 6 feet Must be inspected prior to use Safety line must be able to support 5000 lbs Personal Fall Arrest System, PFAS Slide 9 Guardrails Top rail betwe