ill discuss: Conditions that required use of fall protection Options available to protect workers Slide 5 Fall Protection Falls are the leading cause", "topicId": "topic-1-10", "keyTerms": ["fall", "slide", "protection", "construction", "must", "safety"], "priority": "understand", "likelyDoubts": ["When fall protection is needed?"], "estimatedMinutes": 5}, "targetConcepts": ["MODULE 3"], "scopeBoundaries": ["Ground every document claim in extracted pages the student can access.", "Cite exact page numbers and do not reconstruct missing or unextracted content."], "knownFailureDetails": [], "learningPathPosition": null}	{"door": "uploaded_document", "label": "Construction Safety, Tunnelling & Ergonomics — Module 3 · selected pages", "intentId": "39429b90-a103-41a8-ad6e-a706dc586ee5", "citations": [{"page": 1, "label": "Construction Safety, Tunnelling & Ergonomics — Module 3 (p. 1–10)", "excerpt": "Slide 1 MODULE 3 Slide 2 Major hazards of construction Falls Electrocution Being struck by falling objects Trapped during excavation Slide 3 Major hazards of construction 1 Slide 4 Fall Protection This section will discuss: Conditions that required use of fall protection Options available to protect workers Slide 5 Fall Protection Falls are the leading cause of fatalities in the construction industry Conditions that required use of fall protection A fall from as little as 4-6 feet Can cause loss of work In some cases death Slide 6 When fall protection is needed? Walkways & ramps Open sides & edges Holes Concrete forms & rebar Excavations Roofs Wall openings Bricklaying Residential Construction Slide 7 Safety Nets Hand Rails Safety Harness (PFAS) Equipment guards Fall protection systems must be in place before work start Fall protection and prevention options Slide 8 Must be properly trained Key requirements No free fall more than 6 feet Must be inspected prior to use Safety line must be able to support 5000 lbs Personal Fall Arrest System, PFAS Slide 9 Guardrails Top rail between 39 to 45 inches tall Toeboards at least 3 inches tall Top rail Mid Rail Toe board Slide 10 Safety Nets", "section": "MODULE 3", "version": "1:76ab940581b91568", "sourceId": "3d013096-89b2-42cb-b638-88e6f3c75362", "sourceType": "document"}], "pageIndex": 0, "returnHref": "/materials", "referenceId": "3d013096-89b2-42cb-b638-88e6f3c75362", "resumeToken": "33d5b676-098d-40cb-b7c7-ab33c3ffef01", "returnLabel": "Study materials", "registerFactors": ["The student explicitly asked to be taught from their own uploaded pages.", "Keep source grounding visible and separate explanation from quotation."], "emotionalRegister": "curious_grounded", "policyDisclosures": ["Petros uses only extracted pages available to this student and cites their page numbers.", "Document teaching is for explanation and limited quotation; the original file remains the source."], "sourceFingerprint": "8e59b693504a938cff44140a5313b9e67df75df9f90a1a133326871433e99223", "institutionalDecision": {"homework": null, "syllabus": {"decision": "within_bounds", "allowedDepth": 3, "teachingShape": "normal", "requestedTopic": "MODULE 3", "sourceBoundary": {"approvedSourceIds": [], "externalSourcesAllowed": false}, "studentMessage": "This topic is inside your institution's approved learning scope."}}}	stage4-plan-v1	petros.plan.v3	\N	student_stopped	2026-09-02 19:18:14.747+03	2	2026-09-02 15:10:27.043029+03	2026-09-02 19:18:14.747+03	cfd4fce6-bd83-4f91-8692-c47ed5899b99	ae6b4a8d-702f-4fab-8cf3-d2044540763b	{"profile": {"cache": {"key": "73ed97715f7b74166a13b9767f1005630a6c08f5f7131bffba94014d5f3eb3c1", "version": 1, "invalidationInputs": {"groundingHash": "ede385b7902d0cc3ac3b551f78fbba5333dbcef07d2bdf02fda74b8b3a7414dd", "promptVersion": "stage4-topic-profile-v1", "schemaVersion": "petros.topic-profile.v1", "syllabusVersion": "unspecified", "curriculumVersion": "unspecified"}}, "identity": {"topicKey": "door:uploaded_document:3d013096-89b2-42cb-b638-88e6f3c75362", "gradeBand": "unspecified", "subjectKey": "m