65d76b35-f04a-49b4-a384-fd4529c64324	35b158b4-828f-4535-82f4-94ff91c82fd2	dc099dcb-289c-476b-99ed-d29d486b7c7b	0cd6f10d-fa83-4df4-a3b4-5d9d202aace5	bb490cc7-c455-4bab-9be2-ec5adb06cac5	continue	completed	{"id": "35b158b4-828f-4535-82f4-94ff91c82fd2", "topic": "Pages 1–3", "blocks": [{"id": "e0fe8741-a96c-4aed-84af-85c6085fd06c", "status": "current", "content": {"type": "layered_explanation", "title": "Understanding Learning from Examples (Pages 1–3)", "keyIdea": "Agents learn from examples to handle unpredictable environments, changing conditions, and tasks where human programmers cannot write explicit rules.", "firstLayer": "According to the course notes on pages 1–3, an agent is considered to be learning when it improves its performance on future tasks after observing the world. Instead of a programmer hardcoding every behavior, learning allows the system to adapt dynamically. The notes outline three core reasons we rely on learning from examples: first, designers cannot anticipate every future situation (like a robot facing a brand-new maze); second, conditions change over time (like adapting to stock market shifts); and third, programmers often do not know how to write a direct solution themselves (such as recognizing family faces).", "deeperLayers": [{"id": "layer-2", "label": "Deeper mechanism: Why programming falls short", "content": "When human intuition or explicit rules cannot capture a complex pattern—like distinguishing subtle facial features—examples act as the ground truth. The algorithm inspects data instances and extracts its own internal rules, shifting the burden from human manual programming to automated pattern discovery."}, {"id": "layer-3", "label": "Connecting to the broader module", "content": "This foundational motivation sets up the rest of Module 5. Once we establish why agents must learn from data, we examine forms of learning, supervised methods, and how to evaluate decision trees and linear models to pick the best hypothesis."}], "schem