ent-led explanation now becomes a visual, editable diagram before any knowledge check. That makes diagram the safest next action.", "delivery": "ai", "sequence": 2, "createdAt": "2026-09-01T19:38:58.268Z", "questions": [], "sessionId": "668cc0e9-85cf-4dd4-b915-542656c986dc", "decisionId": "4efe6667-1833-4d32-b162-8fbb3f391d3f", "completedAt": "2026-09-01T19:39:28.117Z", "correlationId": "36741d10-f6ea-4acf-9ea1-0b01dc8bc800", "observedOutcome": {"stage": 3, "completed": true}, "interactionState": {"diagramExpanded": false, "diagramNodeLabels": {}}}, {"id": "4666a048-0b24-4c24-b7fd-be8ac01e64c3", "status": "current", "content": {"type": "hook", "title": "Why Agents Need to Learn", "anchor": "Real-world agent learning in unmapped or changing environments (Machine Learning — Module 5, p. 1)", "prompt": "Imagine you are building a robot to explore an underground cave system whose exact tunnels nobody has mapped yet. Based on the reasons discussed in your document (Pages 1–3), why is it better for the robot to learn from examples as it explores rather than having human programmers try to write every single movement rule beforehand?", "reveal": "As Page 1 explains, designers cannot anticipate all possible situations (like new maze layouts), conditions change over time (like shifting stock markets), and sometimes programmers simply do not know how to code a solution themselves (like face recognition). Learning lets agents adapt where hardcoded rules fall short.", "schemaVersion": "petros.render.v2"}, "version": 0, "whyThis": "A brief grounded hook re-engages the core idea of agent learning from the document context before checking understanding.", "delivery": "ai", "sequence": 3, "createdAt": "2026-09-01T19:39:28.186Z", "questions": [], "sessionId": "668cc0e9-85cf-4dd4-b915-542656c986dc", "decisionId": "10d8471a-9b68-4957-8bff-484c1bcc0d4e", "completedAt": null, "correlationId": "31277cfb-38e6-4976-a380-4c8cf6f7a471", "observedOutcome": null, "interactionState": {"revealed": false}}], "status": "active", "detours": [], "version": 2, "maxMoves": 5, "createdAt": "2026-09-01T19:38:31.802Z", "updatedAt": "2026-09-01T19:39:35.214Z", "checkpoints": [{"id": "checkpoint-1", "label": "Find the useful entry", "status": "complete"}, {"id": "checkpoint-2", "label": "Build the idea", "status": "complete"}, {"id": "checkpoint-3", "label": "Bring it back", "status": "current"}, {"id": "checkpoint-4", "label": "Make it yours", "status": "pending"}, {"id": "checkpoint-5", "label": "Close it out", "status": "pending"}], "closeReason": null, "currentGoal": "Build a clear working understanding of Pages 1–3, then check it with one explanation or example.", "sessionType": "learn", "stateVersion": "petros.session-state.v3", "topicProfile": {"cacheKey": "1fb67feed046b58e234b3845cccd0f186e1dfae2cc0dc9caeeaf4f2eca94802a", "profileId": "5a874d29-3554-4111-b4c1-b8974698959e", "resolution": "canonical", "knowledgeTypes": ["conceptual"], "overrideVersion": null, "representations": ["verbal", "comparative"], "canonicalVersion": 1, "canonicalVersionId": "80b749f7-232e-4b96-accd-98229252eed0"}, "sourceContext": {"door": "uploaded_document", "label": "Machine Learning — Module 5: Learning from Examples · selected pages", "intentId": "5c0dbabc-74b0-4497-88e8-bda7b9f57a25", "citations": [{"page": 1, "label": "Machine Learning — Module 5: Learning from Examples (p. 1–3)", "excerpt": "Module - 5 (Machine Learning) Syllabus Learning from Examples – Forms of Learning, Supervised Learning, Learning Decision Trees, Evaluating and choosing the best hypothesis, Regression and classification with Linear models. Q) Explain why would we want an agent to learn with example . • An agent is learning if it improves its performance on future tasks after making observations about the world. • Why would we want an agent to learn? If the design of the agent can be improved, why wouldn’t the designers just program in that improvement to begin wit h? • There are three main reasons. • First, the designers cannot anticipate all possible situations that the agent might find itself in. For example, a robot designed to navigate mazes must learn the layout of each new maze it encounters. • Second, the design ers cannot anticipate all changes over time; a program designed to predict tomorrow’s stock market prices must learn to adapt when conditions change from boom to bust. • Third, sometimes human programmers have no idea how to program a solution themselves. F or example, most people are good at recognizing the faces of family members, but even the best programmers are unable to progra", "section": "Pages 1–3", "version": "1:46b63aeb22c661ff", "sourceId": "136cd389-8be9-4f12-937f-8bcbc5acf96a", "sourceType": "document"}], "pageIndex": 0, "returnHref": "/materials", "referenceId": "136cd389-8be9-4f12-937f-8bcbc5acf96a", "resumeToken": "7f58aab8-a30f-4b4f-a891-892a5fc8f20c", "returnLabel": "Study materials", "registerFactors": ["The student explicitly asked to be taught from their own uploaded pages.", "Keep source grounding visible and separate explanation from quotation."], "emotionalRegister": "curious_grounded", "policyDisclosures": ["Petros uses only extracted pages available to this student and cites their page numbers.", "Document teaching is for explanation and limited quotation; the original file remains the source."], "sourceFingerprint": "1c2d133496a680102f7c074632fdda9a7075613e061891c76891c2abbdbbb1af", "institutionalDecision": {"homework": null, "syllabus": {"decision": "within_bounds", "allowedDepth": 3, "teachingShape": "normal", "requestedTopic": "Pages 1–3", "sourceBoundary": {"approvedSourceIds": [], "externalSourcesAllowed": false}, "studentMessage": "This topic is inside your institution's approved learning scope."}}}, "elapsedSeconds": 62, "fallbackNotice": null, "personalization": {"status": "neutral", "reasons": ["deep depth and deliberate pace are supported by 19 observed actions.", "Confidence is still fragile across 10 checked answ