2bb3f080-51b5-4e8d-8b58-3c2ef2da2afb	0418f4ad-871b-401d-a11b-6d3a77621e83	dc099dcb-289c-476b-99ed-d29d486b7c7b	0cd6f10d-fa83-4df4-a3b4-5d9d202aace5	4b2aba6a-3327-4447-8af0-9f6b14f58594	continue	completed	{"id": "0418f4ad-871b-401d-a11b-6d3a77621e83", "topic": "MODULE 3", "blocks": [{"id": "8c1de114-6c53-46ea-8da1-a6c7df85891f", "status": "current", "content": {"type": "layered_explanation", "title": "MODULE 3: Construction Hazards & Fall Protection", "keyIdea": "Falls are the leading cause of construction fatalities, and proper safety systems (like guardrails, nets, and personal arrest equipment) must be in place before any work starts.", "firstLayer": "Module 3 outlines the major physical hazards found on construction sites—specifically falls, electrocution, struck-by incidents, and excavation entrapment. Among these, falls are singled out as the leading cause of fatalities in the industry. Even a relatively short drop of 4 to 6 feet can result in severe injuries, lost work time, or fatalities, which makes identifying hazard areas and implementing proper fall protection before work begins essential.", "deeperLayers": [{"id": "when-needed", "label": "When Fall Protection is Needed", "content": "Protection must be actively established whenever workers face exposure to open sides, edges, walkways, ramps, holes, roofs, wall openings, concrete forms, rebar, bricklaying operations, excavations, and residential construction zones."}, {"id": "systems-and-equipment", "label": "Protection Options & Requirements", "content": "Options range from safety nets and handrails to equipment guards and Personal Fall Arrest Systems (PFAS). When using a PFAS, workers must be properly trained, inspect gear prior to use, ensure safety lines support at least 5,000 pounds, and rig systems so there is never a free fall exceeding 6 feet. Guardrails are another standard control, requiring a top rail between 39 and 45 inches high and toeboards at least 3 inches tall."}], "schemaVersion": "petro