rate molecules.", "keyTerms": ["build", "calvin", "carbohydrate", "carbon", "cycle", "dioxide"], "pageStart": 3, "likelyDoubts": ["How does Page 3 fit into the rest of the document?"], "estimatedMinutes": 5}], "deadline": null, "outlineStatus": "generated", "outlineCacheKey": "99e07116-0056-483f-bfb5-00c3fb24e6d3:2:80c688ed7434c6c65021", "suggestedTopicId": "topic-1-1", "suggestedPriority": "understand", "estimatedTotalMinutes": 15}, "citations": [{"page": 1, "label": "Science Fundamentals Notes (p. 1)", "excerpt": "Photosynthesis stores light energy as chemical energy. Chlorophyll absorbs light most strongly in the blue and red regions.", "section": "Page 1", "version": "2:80c688ed7434c6c6", "sourceId": "99e07116-0056-483f-bfb5-00c3fb24e6d3", "sourceType": "document"}, {"page": 2, "label": "Science Fundamentals Notes (p. 2)", "excerpt": "In the light-dependent reactions, water is split and oxygen is released. ATP and NADPH carry energy to the next stage.", "section": "Page 2", "version": "2:80c688ed7434c6c6", "sourceId": "99e07116-0056-483f-bfb5-00c3fb24e6d3", "sourceType": "document"}, {"page": 3, "label": "Science Fundamentals Notes (p. 3)", "excerpt": "The Calvin cycle uses carbon dioxide, ATP, and NADPH to build carbohydrate molecules. It does not directly require light.", "section": "Page 3", "version": "2:80c688ed7434c6c6", "sourceId": "99e07116-0056-483f-bfb5-00c3fb24e6d3", "sourceType": "document"}], "createdAt": "2026-09-01T17:27:19.411Z", "expiresAt": "2026-09-01T17:57:19.411Z", "studentId": "0cd6f10d-fa83-4df4-a3b4-5d9d202aace5", "sessionType": "learn", "accessibility": [], "correlationId": "b1666cb0-098f-48b6-bc4d-3476413dc8c9", "institutionId": "dc099dcb-289c-476b-99ed-d29d486b7c7b", "schemaVersion": "petros.session-intent.v1", "idempotencyKey": "530febdd-abd5-49ff-b30d-04e795ba786d", "targetConcepts": ["Page 1", "Page 2", "Page 3"], "homeworkContext": null, "registerFactors": ["The student explicitly asked to be taught from their own uploaded pages.",