es Pages 1–3 fit into the rest of the document?"], "estimatedMinutes": 11}, {"id": "topic-4-6", "title": "Pages 4–6", "pageEnd": 6, "summary": "3. Semi - Supervised Learning Semi - Supervised learning is a type of Machine Learning algorithm that lies between Supervised and Unsupervised machine learning .", "keyTerms": ["learning", "data", "supervised", "unsupervised", "actions", "sequence"], "pageStart": 4, "likelyDoubts": ["How does Pages 4–6 fit into the rest of the document?"], "estimatedMinutes": 11}, {"id": "topic-7-9", "title": "Pages 7–9", "pageEnd": 9, "summary": "In linear regression(univariate regression) the aim is to draw a best fit line through the data that captures relation between independent and dependent variables with least residual error.", "keyTerms": ["data", "example", "training", "hypothesis", "fitting", "polynomial"], "pageStart": 7, "likelyDoubts": ["When the output y is one of a finite set of values (such as sunny, cloudy or rainy ), the learning problem is called classification , and is called Boolean or binary classificati on if there are only two values.", "When y is a number (such as tomorrow’s temperature), the learning problem is called regression .", "When the output y is one of a finite set of values (such as sunny, cloudy or rainy ), the learning problem is called classification , and is called Boolean or binary classification CLASSIFICATION if there are only two values."], "estimatedMinutes": 19}, {"id": "topic-10-12", "title": "Pages 10–12", "pageEnd": 12, "summary": "Q) explain Linear regr ession /Univariate regression Q) explain gradient de scent method for finding best fit line in linear regression Q) types of gradient descent methods Linear regression (Univariate regression) using gradient descent A univariate linear function (a straight line) with input x and output y has the form y = w 1 x + w 0 , where w 0 and w 1", "keyTerms": ["loss", "linear", "function", "regression", "gradient", "descent"], "pageStart": 10, "likelyDoubts": ["How does Pages 10–12 fit into the rest of the document?"], "estimatedMinutes": 20}, {"id": "topic-13-15", "title": "Pages 13–15", "pageEnd": 15, "summary": "Q) explain various types of regression Types of Regression • Univariate ( linear and polynomial) • M ultivariate or multiple regression Prepared By Abin Philip, Asst Prof, Toc H.", "keyTerms": ["regression", "logistic", "linear", "used", "learning", "variable"], "pageStart": 13, "likelyDoubts": ["How does Pages 13–15 fit into the rest of the document?"], "estimatedMinutes": 8}, {"id": "topic-16-18", "title": "Pages 16–18", "pageEnd": 18, "summary": "o The sigmoid function is a mathemat ical function used to map the predicted values to probabilities.", "keyTerms": ["table", "animal", "aquatic", "birth", "gives", "class"], "pageStart": 16, "likelyDoubts": ["How does Pages 16–18 fit into the rest of the document?"], "estimatedMinutes": 16}, {"id": "topic-19-21", "title": "Pages 19–21", "pageEnd": 21, "summary": "Step 3 Next we consider the examples in Table 8.3 and split them into disjoint subsets based on the values of “aquatic animal”.", "keyTerms": ["class", "table", "tree", "animal", "aquatic", "data"], "pageStart": 19, "likelyDoubts": ["How does Pages 19–21 fit into the rest of the document?"], "estimatedMinutes": 18}, {"id": "topic-22-24", "title": "Pages 22–24", "pageEnd": 24, "summary": "Let “i” be some class label. We denote by p i the proportion of examples with class label “i”.", "keyTerms": ["entropy", "table", "fish", "bird", "class", "gain"], "pageStart": 22, "likelyDoubts": ["How does Pages 22–24 fit into the rest of the document?"], "estimatedMinutes": 20}, {"id": "topic-25-27", "title": "Pages 25–27", "pageEnd": 27, "summary": "Gini indices The Gini split index of a data set is another feature selection measure in the construction of classification trees.", "keyTerms": ["decision", "tree", "gini", "values", "examples", "data"], "pageStart": 25, "likelyDoubts": ["What is Pruning?"], "estimatedMinutes": 13}, {"id": "topic-28-30", "title": "Pages 28–30", "pageEnd": 30, "summary": "Another com plete solved example can be foun d at - https://kindsonthegenius.com/blog/how - to - build - a - decision - tree - for - classification - step - by - step - procedure - using - entrop y - and - gain/ Visit www.sharikatr.in for my notes and ppts So 𝐺𝑎𝑖𝑛 𝑆, 𝐴 1 = ES − { 𝑆 𝐴1=1 𝑆 𝐸𝑛𝑡𝑟𝑜𝑝𝑦 𝑆 𝐴1=1 + 𝑆 𝐴1=0 𝑆 𝐸𝑛𝑡𝑟𝑜𝑝𝑦 𝑆 𝐴1=0", "keyTerms": ["data", "test", "hypothesis", "model", "training", "validation"], "pageStart": 28, "likelyDoubts": ["How does Pages 28–30 fit into the rest of the document?"], "estimatedMinutes": 11}, {"id": "topic-31-33", "title": "Pages 31–33", "pageEnd": 33, "summary": "From error rates to loss • So far, we have been trying to minimize error rate.", "keyTerms": ["loss", "error", "examples", "function", "data", "hypothesis"], "pageStart": 31, "likelyDoubts": ["When the output y is one of a finite set of values (such as sunny, cloudy or rainy ), the learning problem is called classification , and is called Boolean or binary classification CLASSIFICATION if there are only two values."], "estimatedMinutes": 20}], "deadline": null, "outlineStatus": "cached", "outlineCacheKey": "136cd389-8be9-4f12-937f-8bcbc5acf96a:1:46b63aeb22c661ff746c", "suggestedTopicId": "topic-1-3", "suggestedPriority": "understand", "estimatedTotalMinutes": 167}, "citations": [{"page": 1, "label": "Machine Learning — Module 5: Learning from Examples (p. 1–3)", "excerpt": "Module - 5 (Machine Learning) Syllabus Learning from Examples – Forms of Learning, Supervised Learning, Learning Decision Trees, Evaluating and choosing the best hypothesis, Regression and classification with Linear models. Q) Explain why would we want an agent to learn with example . • An agent is learning if it improves its performance on future tasks after making observations about the world. • Why would we want an agent to learn? If the design of the agent can be improved, why wouldn’t the designers just program in that improvement to begin wit h? • There are three main reasons. • First, the designers cannot anticipate all possible situations that the agent might find itself in. For example, a robot designed to navigate mazes must learn the layout of each new maze it encounters. • Second, the design ers cannot anticipate all changes over time; a program designed to predict tomorrow’s stock market prices must learn to adapt when conditions change from boom to bust. • Third, sometimes human programmers have no idea how to program a solution themselves. F or example, most people are good at recognizing the faces of family members, but even the best programmers are unable to progra", "section": "Pages 1–3", "version": "1:46b63aeb22c661ff", "sourceId": "136cd389-8be9-4f12-937f-8bcbc5acf96a", "sourceType": "document"}, {"page": 4, "label": "Machine Learning — Module 5: Learning from Examples (p. 4–6)", "excerpt": "3. Semi - Supervised Learning Semi - Supervised learning is a type of Machine Learning algorithm that lies between Supervised and Unsupervised machine learning . It represents the i ntermediate ground between Supervised (With Labelled training data) and Unsupervised learning (with no labelled training data) algorithms and uses the combination of labelled and unlabeled datasets during the training period. A lthough Semi - supervised learn ing is the middle ground between supervised and unsupervised learning and operates on the data that consists of a few labels, it mostly consists of unlabeled data. As labels are costly, but for corporate purposes, they may have few labels. It is completely different from supervised and unsupervised learning as they are based on the presence & absence of labels. To overcome the drawbacks of supervised learning and unsupervised learning algorithms, the concept of Semi - supervised learning is introduced . The main aim of semi - supervised learning is to effectively use all the available data, rather than only labelled data like in supervised learning. Initially, similar data is cl ustered along with an unsupervised learning algorithm, and further, it", "section": "Pages 4–6", "version": "1:46b63aeb22c661ff", "sourceId": "136cd389-8be9-4f12-937f-8bcbc5acf96a", "sourceType": "document"}, {"page": 7, "label": "Machine Learning — Module 5: Learning from Examples (p. 7–9)", "excerpt": "In linear regression(univariate regression) the aim is to draw a best fit line through the data that captures relation between independent and dependent variables with least residual error. The above figure shows an example for binary classification. The purpose of the classifier(line) is to classify the data into two classes - class A and class B. S UPERVISED L EARNING Q) explain the concept o f hyposthesis Q) Explain Ockham’s razor concept The task of supervised learning is this: Given a training set of N example input – output pairs ( x 1 ,y 1 ) , ( x 2 ,y 2 ) ,. .. ( x N ,y N ) , where each y j was generated by an unknown function y = f ( x ) , discover a function h that approximates the true function f . Here x and y can be any value; they need not be numbers. The function h is a hypothesis . Learning is a search through the space of possible hypotheses for one that will perform well, even on new examples beyond the training set. To measure the accuracy of a hypothesis we give it a test set of examples that are distinct from the training set. The hypothesis generalizes well if it correctly predicts the value of y for novel examples. When the output y is one of a finite set of", "section": "Pages 7–9", "version": "1:46b63aeb22c661ff", "sourceId": "136cd389-8be9-4f12-937f-8bcbc5acf96a", "sourceType": "document"}, {"page": 10, "label": "Machine Learning — Module 5: Learning from Examples (p. 10–12)", "excerpt": "Q) explain Linear regr ession /Univariate regression Q) explain gradient de scent method for finding best fit line in linear regression Q) types of gradient descent methods Linear regression (Univariate regression) using gradient descent A univariate linear function (a straight line) with input x and output y has the form y = w 1 x + w 0 , where w 0 and w 1 are real - valued coefficients to be learne d. Example Above Figure shows an example of a training set of n points in the x, y plane, each point representing the size in square feet and the price of a house offered for sale. The task of finding the h w that best fits these data is called linear regression . To fit a line to the data, all we have to do is find the values of the weights [ w 0 , w 1 ] that minimize the empirical loss (error) . 718 Chapter 18. Learning from Examples 300 400 500 600 700 800 900 1000 500 1000 1500 2000 2500 3000 3500 House price in $1000 House size in square feet w 0 w 1 Loss (a) (b) Figure 18.13 (a) Data points of price versus floor space of houses for sale in Berkeley, CA, in July 2009, along with the linear function hypothesis that minimizes squared error loss: y = 0 . 232 x + 246 . (b) Plot of the", "section": "Pages 10–12", "version": "1:46b63aeb22c661ff", "sourceId": "136cd389-8be9-4f12-937f-8bcbc5acf96a", "sourceType": "document"}, {"page": 13, "label": "Machine Learning — Module 5: Learning from Examples (p. 13–15)", "excerpt": "Q) explain various types of regression Types of Regression • Univariate ( linear and polynomial) • M ultivariate or multiple regression Prepared By Abin Philip, Asst Prof, Toc H. Reference: Introduction to Machine Learning, II edition, Ethem Alpaydin 21 The MLE that we find by taking the partial derivatives of the log likelihood and setting them equal to 0 are 3.10 Regression A regression problem is the problem of determining a relation between one or more independent variables and an output variable which is a real continuous variable, given a set of observed values of the set of independent variables and the corres ponding values of the output variable. 2 2 σ 2 The MLE that we fi nd by taking the p artial d e rivatives o f the log l ikeli- ho o d and setting them equal to 0 are m = t x t N (4.8) s 2 = t ( x t − m ) 2 N L ( μ , σ | X ) = − 2 lo g ( 2 π ) − N lo g σ − t 2 σ 2 The MLE that we fi nd by taking the p artial d e rivatives o f the log l ikeli- ho o d and setting them equal to 0 are m = t x t N (4.8) s 2 = t ( x t − m ) 2 N APJKTU, CS 476 Introduction to Machine Learning , Module 3 Polynomial regression Q) Explai n Logistic Regression with example Linear classification wi", "section": "Pages 13–15", "version": "1:46b63aeb22c661ff", "sourceId": "136cd389-8be9-4f12-937f-8bcbc5acf96a", "sourceType": "document"}, {"page": 16, "label": "Machine Learning — Module 5: Learning from Examples (p. 16–18)", "excerpt": "o The sigmoid function is a mathemat ical function used to map the predicted values to probabilities. o It maps any real value into another value within a range of 0 and 1. o The value of the logistic regression must be between 0 and 1, which cannot go beyond this limit, so it forms a curve like the \\"S\\" form. The S - form curve is called the Sigmoid function or the logistic function. o In logistic regression, we use the concept of the threshold value, which defines the probability of either 0 or 1. Such as values above the threshold value tends to 1, and a value below the threshold values tends to 0. Decision Trees Q)Explain deci sion tree method/algorithm with suitable example Decision Trees are a type of Supervised Machine Learning where the data is continuously split according to a certain parameter. The tree can be explained by two entities, namely decision nodes and leaves. The leaves are the decisions or the final outcomes. And the decision nodes are where the data is split. An example of a deci sion tree can be explained using above binary tree. DEPARTMENT OF CSE SNGCE 59 Visit https://www.youtube.com/c/sharikatr for my video lectures Visit https://www.youtube.com/c/sharikat", "section": "Pages 16–18", "version": "1:46b63aeb22c661ff", "sourceId": "136cd389-8be9-4f12-937f-8bcbc5acf96a", "sourceType": "document"}, {"page": 19, "label": "Machine Learning — Module 5: Learning from Examples (p. 19–21)", "excerpt": "Step 3 Next we consider the examples in Table 8.3 and split them into disjoint subsets based on the values of “aquatic animal”. We get the examples in Table 8.6 for “yes”, the examp les in Table 8.8 for “no” and the examples in Table 8.7 for “semi”. We now split the resulting subsets based on the values of “has legs”, etc. Putting all these together, we get the the diagram in tree (Figure 8.5) for the data in Table 8.1 Remarks about Decision Trees • Elements of a classification tree The various elements in a classification tree are identified as follows. animal animal bel salmon no yes no no fish Table 8.6: The vertebrate data set Name gives birth aquatic animal aerial animal has legs Class la- bel frog no semi no yes amphibian turtle no semi no yes amphibian salamander no semi no yes amphibian Table 8.7: The vertebrate data set Name gives birth aquatic animal aerial animal has legs Class la- bel python no no no no reptile pigeon no no yes yes bird Table 8.8: The vertebrate data set “has legs”, etc. Putting all these together, we get the the diagram in Figure 8.5 as the classification tree for the data in Table 8.1. 8.3.2 Classification tree in rule format The classification tree s", "section": "Pages 19–21", "version": "1:46b63aeb22c661ff", "sourceId": "136cd389-8be9-4f12-937f-8bcbc5acf96a", "sourceType": "document"}, {"page": 22, "label": "Machine Learning — Module 5: Learning from Examples (p. 22–24)", "excerpt": "Let “i” be some class label. We denote by p i the proportion of examples with class label “i”. 1. Let S be the data in Table 8.1. The class labels are ”amphi”, “bird”, ”fish”, ”mammal” and ”reptile”. In S we have the following numbers. Number of examples with class label “amphi” =3 Number of examples with class label “bird” =2 Number of examples wit h class label “fish” =2 Number of examples with class label “mammal” =2 Number of examples with class label “reptile” =1 Total number of examples Therefore, we have: Entropy (S) = ∑ for all classes “i” − p i log 2 (p i ) Similarly, Entropy for following table 8.1 (table with Gives Birth = Yes) Entropy for following table 8.2 (Table with gives birth = No) N TREES 91 = − p amphi log 2 ( p amphi ) − p bird log 2 ( p bird ) − p fish log 2 ( p fish ) − p mammal log 2 ( p mammal ) − p reptile log 2 ( p reptile ) = − ( 3 ￿ 10 ) log 2 ( 3 ￿ 10 ) − ( 2 ￿ 10 ) log 2 ( 2 ￿ 10 ) − ( 2 ￿ 10 ) log 2 ( 2 ￿ 10 ) − ( 2 ￿ 10 ) log 2 ( 2 ￿ 10 ) − ( 1 ￿ 10 ) log 2 ( 1 ￿ 10 ) = 2 . 2464 in Table 8.2 ment S of the data in Table 8.1 given in Table 8.2. For quick reference, the roduced below: e Gives birth Aquatic animal Aerial animal Has legs Class la- bel n", "section": "Pages 22–24", "version": "1:46b63aeb22c661ff", "sourceId": "136cd389-8be9-4f12-937f-8bcbc5acf96a", "sourceType": "document"}, {"page": 25, "label": "Machine Learning — Module 5: Learning from Examples (p. 25–27)", "excerpt": "Gini indices The Gini split index of a data set is another feature selection measure in the construction of classification trees. This measure is used in the CART algorithm Q) Explain the Issues in decision trees and i ts solutions Q) Explain overfitting in decision trees and how to overcome it (tree pr uning methods) Q) Explai n the iss ue of missing attributes with example, how is it solved. • Generalization and overfitting • Issues with missing attributes Decision Trees are a non - parametric supervised machine learning approach for classification and regression tasks. D ECISION - T REE - L EARNING algorithm will generate a large tree when there is actually no pattern to be found. Overfitting ref ers to the condition when the model completely fits the training data but fails to generalize the testing unseen data. (can draw fig and use explanation of overfitting in previous section here) Overfit condition arises when the model memorizes the noise of the training data and fails to capture important patterns. A perfectly fit decision tree performs well for training data but performs poorly for unseen test data. If the decision tree is allowed to train to its full strength, the mode", "section": "Pages 25–27", "version": "1:46b63aeb22c661ff", "sourceId": "136cd389-8be9-4f12-937f-8bcbc5acf96a", "sourceType": "document"}, {"page": 28, "label": "Machine Learning — Module 5: Learning from Examples (p. 28–30)", "excerpt": "Another com plete solved example can be foun d at - https://kindsonthegenius.com/blog/how - to - build - a - decision - tree - for - classification - step - by - step - procedure - using - entrop y - and - gain/ Visit www.sharikatr.in for my notes and ppts So 𝐺𝑎𝑖𝑛 𝑆, 𝐴 1 = ES − { 𝑆 𝐴1=1 𝑆 𝐸𝑛𝑡𝑟𝑜𝑝𝑦 𝑆 𝐴1=1 + 𝑆 𝐴1=0 𝑆 𝐸𝑛𝑡𝑟𝑜𝑝𝑦 𝑆 𝐴1=0 } = 1 − 3 6 𝑋0 .9183 + 3 6 𝑋 0.9183 = 0.0817 ----- (1) DEPARTMENT OF CSE SNGCE 68 |S A2=1 |=4 Entropy(S A2=1 )= -P 1 log 2 P 1 - P 0 log 2 P 0 = − 2 4 𝑙𝑜𝑔 2 2 4 − 2 4 𝑙𝑜𝑔 2 2 4 = 1 |S A2=0 |=2 Entropy(S A1=0 )= − 1 2 𝑙𝑜𝑔 2 1 2 − 1 2 𝑙𝑜𝑔 2 1 2 = 1 DEPARTMENT OF CSE SNGCE 69 X A2 Y X1 1 1 X2 1 1 X5 1 0 X6 1 0 X A2 Y X3 0 0 X4 0 1 S A2=1 S A2=0 Visit https://www.youtube.com/c/sharikatr for my video lectures Visit www.sharikatr.in for my notes and ppts Visit www.sharikatr.in for my notes and ppts So 𝐺𝑎𝑖𝑛 𝑆, 𝐴 2 = ES − { 𝑆 𝐴2=1 𝑆 𝐸𝑛𝑡𝑟𝑜𝑝𝑦 𝑆 𝐴2=1 + 𝑆 𝐴2=0 𝑆 𝐸𝑛𝑡𝑟𝑜𝑝𝑦 𝑆 𝐴2=0 } = 1 − 4 6 𝑋 1 + 2 6 𝑋 1 = 0 ---------- (2) DEPARTMENT OF CSE SNGCE 70 From (1) and (2) We found that A1 give more information gain. So we make A1 as root node DEPARTMENT OF CSE SNGCE 71 A1 A2 1 0 A2 1 1 0 0 1 0 0 1 X A2", "section": "Pages 28–30", "version": "1:46b63aeb22c661ff", "sourceId": "136cd389-8be9-4f12-937f-8bcbc5acf96a", "sourceType": "document"}, {"page": 31, "label": "Machine Learning — Module 5: Learning from Examples (p. 31–33)", "excerpt": "From error rates to loss • So far, we have been trying to minimize error rate. This is clearly better than maximizing error rate, this is not enough, • Consider the problem of classifying email messages as spam or non - spam. It is worse to classify non - spam as spam (and thus potentially miss an importa nt message) then to classify spam as non - spam (and thus suffer a few seconds of annoyance). So a classifier with a 1% error rate, where almost all the errors were classifying spam as non - spam, would be better than a classifier with only a 0.5% error rate, if most of those errors were classifying non - spam as spam. decision - makers should maximize expected utility, and utility is what learners should maximize as well. In 696 Chapter 18. Learning from Examples (c) (a) (b) (d) x x x x f ( x ) f ( x ) f ( x ) f ( x ) Figure 18.1 (a) Example ( x, f ( x )) pairs and a consistent, linear hypothesis. (b) A con- sistent, degree-7 polynomial hypothesis for the same data set. (c) A different data set, which admits an exact degree-6 polynomial fit or an approximate linear fit. (d) A simple, exact sinusoidal fit to the same data set. generalizes well if it correctly predicts the value of", "section": "Pages 31–33", "version": "1:46b63aeb22c661ff", "sourceId": "136cd389-8be9-4f12-937f-8bcbc5acf96a", "sourceType": "document"}], "createdAt": "2026-09-02T08:30:33.157Z", "expiresAt": "2026-09-02T09:00:33.149Z", "studentId": "0cd6f10d-fa83-4df4-a3b4-5d9d202aace5", "sessionType": "learn", "accessibility": [], "correlationId": "5ff02a73-201b-4876-9aaf-d1822408512a", "institutionId": "dc099dcb-289c-476b-99ed-d29d486b7c7b", "schemaVersion": "petros.session-intent.v1", "idempotencyKey": "f3424737-70fb-414e-b656-e7ac78597f3d", "targetConcepts": ["Pages 1–3", "Pages 4–6", "Pages 7–9", "Pages 10–12", "Pages 13–15", "Pages 16–18", "Pages 19–21", "Pages 22–24", "Pages 25–27", "Pages 28–30", "Pages 31–33"], "homeworkContext": null, "registerFactors": ["The student explicitly asked to be taught from their own uploaded pages.", "Keep source grounding visible and separate explanation from quotation."], "sourceEntityIds": ["136cd389-8be9-4f12-937f-8bcbc5acf96a", "ece08150-f9d5-491a-9f