if it can be done in another way that avoids the need to enter.", "Why is ergonomics important?"], "knownFailureDetails": [], "learningPathPosition": null}, "status": "resolved", "campusId": "f04d355d-2e1f-4940-9d82-42ad644668f6", "language": "en", "planning": {"topics": [{"id": "topic-1-10", "title": "MODULE 3", "pageEnd": 10, "summary": "Slide 1 MODULE 3 Slide 2 Major hazards of construction Falls Electrocution Being struck by falling objects Trapped during excavation Slide 3 Major hazards of construction 1 Slide 4 Fall Protection This section will discuss: Conditions that required use of fall protection Options available to protect workers Slide 5 Fall Protection Falls are the leading cause", "keyTerms": ["fall", "slide", "protection", "construction", "must", "safety"], "pageStart": 1, "likelyDoubts": ["When fall protection is needed?"], "estimatedMinutes": 5}, {"id": "topic-11-20", "title": "SUMMARY", "pageEnd": 20, "summary": "Slide 11 Falling Objects Hardhats are required Use of canopies is authorized Barricade the area to prevent unauthorized entry Slide 12 SUMMARY A fall of 6 ft or more protection is needed Use fall protection on: Walkways, ramps, open sides, edges, excavations, Slide 13 13 ARE YOU WORKING ON A TRENCH OR DIGGING YOUR GRAVE? Slide 14 TRENCHING & EXCAVATIONHAZARD", "keyTerms": ["slide", "excavation", "employees", "protection", "system", "cave-ins"], "pageStart": 11, "likelyDoubts": ["How to protect employees from cave-ins"], "estimatedMinutes": 5}, {"id": "topic-21-30", "title": "SUMMARY", "pageEnd": 30, "summary": "Slide 21 21 Types of ProtectionTrench Shield A trench shield was built around this work area Slide 22 22 Hydraulic Jacks Hydraulic Jacks Easily dropped in place and adjusted Trench pins installed in case of hydraulic failure Slide 23 23 Egress Systems A stairway, ladder, or ramp must be present in excavations that are 4 or more feet deep, and within 25 feet", "keyTerms": ["slide", "excavation", "hazards", "must", "person", "hydraulic"], "pageStart": 21, "likelyDoubts": ["When you can reasonably anticipate an employee will be exposed to hazards."], "estimatedMinutes": 5}, {"id": "topic-31-40", "title": "Slide 31", "pageEnd": 40, "summary": "Basics of Shoring Safety Shoring systems are used to support structures to prevent them from collapsing during construction.", "keyTerms": ["shoring", "slide", "equipment", "safety", "qualified", "system"], "pageStart": 31, "likelyDoubts": ["When installing and using shoring, it’s vital to follow all state, provincial, local, and federal regulations to ensure worker safety. Here are a few general guidelines that cover the basics of shoring safety:"], "estimatedMinutes": 6}, {"id": "topic-41-50", "title": "Slide 41", "pageEnd": 50, "summary": "Slide 42 Slide 43 Slide 44 Slide 45 Slide 46 Slide 47 Slide 48 Slide 49 Slide 50", "keyTerms": ["slide"], "pageStart": 41, "likelyDoubts": ["How does Slide 41 fit into the rest of the document?"], "estimatedMinutes": 5}, {"id": "topic-51-60", "title": "TUNNELLING", "pageEnd": 60, "summary": "Slide 51 Ladder safety Ladders can pose serious potential for a fall if used improperly or in an unsafe manner.", "keyTerms": ["ladder", "ladders", "author", "licensed", "slide", "unknown"], "pageStart": 51, "likelyDoubts": ["What height will I have to reach and work at?", "What should I check before using the ladder?", "What type of material should it be made of?"], "estimatedMinutes": 7}, {"id": "topic-61-70", "title": "GENERAL SAFETY PRECAUTIONS IN TUNNELLING", "pageEnd": 70, "summary": "Slide 61 Slide 62 Slide 63 Slide 64 GENERAL SAFETY PRECAUTIONS IN TUNNELLING Slide 65 Slide 66 Slide 67 BLASTING Blasting is a process of reduction of rocks or hard soil into fragments with the help of explosives.", "keyTerms": ["slide", "blasting", "charge", "precautions", "safety", "debris"], "pageStart": 61, "likelyDoubts": ["How does GENERAL SAFETY PRECAUTIONS IN TUNNELLING fit into the rest of the document?"], "estimatedMinutes": 5}, {"id": "topic-71-80", "title": "SAFETY PRECAUTIONS", "pageEnd": 80, "summary": "Slide 71 Slide 72 Demolition or Dismantling Demolition or dismantling refers to breaking up of buildings , structures either fully or partially.", "keyTerms": ["slide", "demolition", "dismantling", "confined", "during", "materials"], "pageStart": 71, "likelyDoubts": ["How does SAFETY PRECAUTIONS fit into the rest of the document?"], "estimatedMinutes": 5}, {"id": "topic-81-90", "title": "Slide 81", "pageEnd": 90, "summary": "Confined Space Safety Precautions Where possible, avoid entry to confined spaces.", "keyTerms": ["slide", "confined", "precautions", "space", "safety", "contd"], "pageStart": 81, "likelyDoubts": ["Where possible, avoid entry to confined spaces. Establish if the work is really necessary or if it can be done in another way that avoids the need to enter.", "Why is ergonomics important?"], "estimatedMinutes": 5}, {"id": "topic-91-100", "title": "Slide 91", "pageEnd": 100, "summary": "Slide 92 Slide 93 Slide 94 Slide 95 Slide 96 Slide 97 Slide 98 Slide 99 Slide 100", "keyTerms": ["slide"], "pageStart": 91, "likelyDoubts": ["How does Slide 91 fit into the rest of the document?"], "estimatedMinutes": 5}, {"id": "topic-101-110", "title": "Slide 101", "pageEnd": 110, "summary": "Slide 102 Slide 103 Slide 104 Slide 105 Slide 106 Slide 107 Slide 108 Slide 109 Musculoskeletal Disorder Musculoskeletal Disorders or MSDs are injuries and disorders that affect the human body’s movement or musculoskeletal system (i.e.", "keyTerms": ["slide", "syndrome", "disorders", "musculoskeletal", "injury", "blood"], "pageStart": 101, "likelyDoubts": ["How does Slide 101 fit into the rest of the document?"], "estimatedMinutes": 5}, {"id": "topic-111-115", "title": "Slide 111", "pageEnd": 115, "summary": "The Cause of Musculoskeletal Disorders – Exposure to Risk Factors These risk factors can be broken up into two categories: work-related (ergonomic) risk factors individual-related risk factors Slide 112 Slide 113 Work-related Risk Factors There are three primary ergonomic risk factors.", "keyTerms": ["risk", "factors", "poor", "high", "slide", "work"], "pageStart": 111, "likelyDoubts": ["How does Slide 111 fit into the rest of the document?"], "estimatedMinutes": 7}], "deadline": null, "outlineStatus": "cached", "outlineCacheKey": "3d013096-89b2-42cb-b638-88e6f3c75362:1:76ab940581b91568134a", "suggestedTopicId": "topic-1-10", "suggestedPriority": "understand", "estimatedTotalMinutes": 65}, "citations": [{"page": 1, "label": "Construction Safety, Tunnelling & Ergonomics — Module 3 (p. 1–10)", "excerpt": "Slide 1 MODULE 3 Slide 2 Major hazards of construction Falls Electrocution Being struck by falling objects Trapped during excavation Slide 3 Major hazards of construction 1 Slide 4 Fall Protection This section will discuss: Conditions that required use of fall protection Options available to protect workers Slide 5 Fall Protection Falls are the leading cause of fatalities in the construction industry Conditions that required use of fall protection A fall from as little as 4-6 feet Can cause loss of work In some cases death Slide 6 When fall protection is needed? Walkways & ramps Open sides & edges Holes Concrete forms & rebar Excavations Roofs Wall openings Bricklaying Residential Construction Slide 7 Safety Nets Hand Rails Safety Harness (PFAS) Equipment guards Fall protection systems must be in place before work start Fall protection and prevention options Slide 8 Must be properly trained Key requirements No free fall more than 6 feet Must be inspected prior to use Safety line must be able to support 5000 lbs Personal Fall Arrest System, PFAS Slide 9 Guardrails Top rail between 39 to 45 inches tall Toeboards at least 3 inches tall Top rail Mid Rail Toe board Slide 10 Safety Nets", "section": "MODULE 3", "version": "1:76ab940581b91568", "sourceId": "3d013096-89b2-42cb-b638-88e6f3c75362", "sourceType": "document"}, {"page": 11, "label": "Construction Safety, Tunnelling & Ergonomics — Module 3 (p. 11–20)", "excerpt": "Slide 11 Falling Objects Hardhats are required Use of canopies is authorized Barricade the area to prevent unauthorized entry Slide 12 SUMMARY A fall of 6 ft or more protection is needed Use fall protection on: Walkways, ramps, open sides, edges, excavations, Slide 13 13 ARE YOU WORKING ON A TRENCH OR DIGGING YOUR GRAVE? Slide 14 TRENCHING & EXCAVATIONHAZARDS Risks of excavation How to protect employees from cave-ins Factors that pose a hazard to employees working in excavation Role of competent person Slide 15 EXCAVATION HAZARDSRisks Most hazardous construction operation Cave-ins are the greatest risk Most accidents occurred in 5-15 ft deep Slide 16 EXCAVATION HAZARDSEmployee Protection Employees should be protected from caves-in by using a well designed protective system Systems must be able to support expected loads to the system Slide 17 EXCAVATION HAZARDSProtective System Design A well designed system will have a correct design of sloping and benching systems Correct design of support systems Handle materials and equipment Slide 18 EXCAVATION HAZARDSEmployee Protection Protect employees from potential cave-ins Slope or bench sides of excavation Place shields between the side o", "section": "SUMMARY", "version": "1:76ab940581b91568", "sourceId": "3d013096-89b2-42cb-b638-88e6f3c75362", "sourceType": "document"}, {"page": 21, "label": "Construction Safety, Tunnelling & Ergonomics — Module 3 (p. 21–30)", "excerpt": "Slide 21 21 Types of ProtectionTrench Shield A trench shield was built around this work area Slide 22 22 Hydraulic Jacks Hydraulic Jacks Easily dropped in place and adjusted Trench pins installed in case of hydraulic failure Slide 23 23 Egress Systems A stairway, ladder, or ramp must be present in excavations that are 4 or more feet deep, and within 25 feet of the employees Must extend 3FT above excavation This ladder does not meet the requirements of the standard Slide 24 EXCAVATION HAZARDSCompetent Person Must have had specific training in and be knowledgeable about: Soils classification The use of protective systems The requirements of the standard Must be capable of identifying hazards, and authorized to immediately eliminate hazards Slide 25 EXCAVATION HAZARDSCompetent Person A competent person must make daily inspections of excavations, areas around them and protective systems: Before work starts and as needed After rainstorms, high winds or other occurrence which may increase hazards When you can reasonably anticipate an employee will be exposed to hazards. Slide 26 SUMMARY The greatest risk in an excavation is a cave-in. Employees can be protected through sloping, shielding", "section": "SUMMARY", "version": "1:76ab940581b91568", "sourceId": "3d013096-89b2-42cb-b638-88e6f3c75362", "sourceType": "document"}, {"page": 31, "label": "Construction Safety, Tunnelling & Ergonomics — Module 3 (p. 31–40)", "excerpt": "Slide 31 Basics of Shoring Safety Shoring systems are used to support structures to prevent them from collapsing during construction. Shoring is most commonly used during the earliest phases of construction; when walls or structures are undergoing reinforcement, during excavation, or when a nearby structure needs to be demolished (to prevent the shored building from crumbling along with it). Shoring systems usually support either buildings or trenches. For buildings, a shoring system such as piles and lagging support the surrounding loads until the underground levels of the building are completed and can bear the weight of the rest of the building. For trenches, the shoring system steadies the trench walls to prevent cave-ins and keep workers safe. Slide 32 When installing and using shoring, it’s vital to follow all state, provincial, local, and federal regulations to ensure worker safety. Here are a few general guidelines that cover the basics of shoring safety: A qualified person should survey the jobsite for hazards that could cause issues with the shoring system. If hazards are uncovered, they should be corrected as needed. Plan the shoring’s installation in advance. This inclu", "section": "Slide 31", "version": "1:76ab940581b91568", "sourceId": "3d013096-89b2-42cb-b638-88e6f3c75362", "sourceType": "document"}, {"page": 41, "label": "Construction Safety, Tunnelling & Ergonomics — Module 3 (p. 41–50)", "excerpt": "Slide 41 Slide 42 Slide 43 Slide 44 Slide 45 Slide 46 Slide 47 Slide 48 Slide 49 Slide 50", "section": "Slide 41", "version": "1:76ab940581b91568", "sourceId": "3d013096-89b2-42cb-b638-88e6f3c75362", "sourceType": "document"}, {"page": 51, "label": "Construction Safety, Tunnelling & Ergonomics — Module 3 (p. 51–60)", "excerpt": "Slide 51 Ladder safety Ladders can pose serious potential for a fall if used improperly or in an unsafe manner. OSHA estimates that, for general industry companies, falls from ladders account for 20% of fatal and lost workday injuries. This Photo by Unknown Author is licensed under CC BY-SA-NC Slide 52 Ladder setup First, choose the right ladder. Questions to ask What height will I have to reach and work at? What should I check before using the ladder? What type of material should it be made of? These Photos by Unknown Author are licensed under CC BY-SA Slide 53 Ladder setup Ladder inspection Ladder label Skid resistant footing Free of defects Free of slipping hazards This Photo by Unknown Author is licensed under CC BY-SA This Photo by Unknown Author is licensed under CC BY-SA-NC Slide 54 Safe use of ladders Ensuring the ladder is secure Folding ladders Folding or A-frame ladders should be fully extended and the arms locked into place. Be careful of pinch points which are any places where parts come together and present a possibility for injury. Never use a step ladder as an extension ladder This Photo by Unknown Author is licensed under CC BY-SA Slide 55 Safe use of ladders Ensur", "section": "TUNNELLING", "version": "1:76ab940581b91568", "sourceId": "3d013096-89b2-42cb-b638-88e6f3c75362", "sourceType": "document"}, {"page": 61, "label": "Construction Safety, Tunnelling & Ergonomics — Module 3 (p. 61–70)", "excerpt": "Slide 61 Slide 62 Slide 63 Slide 64 GENERAL SAFETY PRECAUTIONS IN TUNNELLING Slide 65 Slide 66 Slide 67 BLASTING Blasting is a process of reduction of rocks or hard soil into fragments with the help of explosives. The blasting operation involves drilling of holes, installation of a detonator and charge, detonating the charge, and removal of debris. Slide 68 Safety Precautions before Blasting Slide 69 Slide 70", "section": "GENERAL SAFETY PRECAUTIONS IN TUNNELLING", "version": "1:76ab940581b91568", "sourceId": "3d013096-89b2-42cb-b638-88e6f3c75362", "sourceType": "document"}, {"page": 71, "label": "Construction Safety, Tunnelling & Ergonomics — Module 3 (p. 71–80)", "excerpt": "Slide 71 Slide 72 Demolition or Dismantling Demolition or dismantling refers to breaking up of buildings , structures either fully or partially. Precautions during demolition has three goals: specifically aimed at safeguarding the personnel on the site. safeguarding of persons not connected with demolition including the general public and the protection of the property likely to be effected by demolition operation. The causes of accidents to workers involved with demolition are fall from heights, falling materials, inadequate access, over-fragile materials etc. Premature collapses due to incorrect dismantling, over loading or excessive pre- weakening feature particularly during demolition. Slide 73 SAFETY PRECAUTIONS Slide 74 Slide 75 Slide 76 Slide 77 Slide 78 Common confined space hazards Slide 79 Before you conduct any work in Confined Spaces Slide 80", "section": "SAFETY PRECAUTIONS", "version": "1:76ab940581b91568", "sourceId": "3d013096-89b2-42cb-b638-88e6f3c75362", "sourceType": "document"}, {"page": 81, "label": "Construction Safety, Tunnelling & Ergonomics — Module 3 (p. 81–90)", "excerpt": "Slide 81 Confined Space Safety Precautions Where possible, avoid entry to confined spaces. Establish if the work is really necessary or if it can be done in another way that avoids the need to enter. If entry to a confined space is unavoidable then you must follow a safe system of work. Have emergency procedures in place before work starts.​ The results of your risk assessment will help you identify the risks and necessary precautions. Slide 82 Confined Space Safety Precautions (contd.) Slide 83 Confined Space Safety Precautions (contd.) Slide 84 Confined Space Safety Precautions (contd.) Slide 85 Confined Space Safety Precautions (contd.) Slide 86 Confined Space Safety Precautions (contd.) Slide 87 Introduction Ergonomics “The scientific discipline concerned with understanding of interactions among humans and other elements of a system, and the profession that applies theory, principles, methods and data to design in order to optimize human well-being and overall system performance” Slide 88 Introduction Ergonomics means “fitting the job to the worker,” including: Work stations Tools Equipment Ergo = Work Nomos = Law Slide 89 Introduction Why is ergonomics important? Overexertion", "section": "Slide 81", "version": "1:76ab940581b91568", "sourceId": "3d013096-89b2-42cb-b638-88e6f3c75362", "sourceType": "document"}, {"page": 91, "label": "Construction Safety, Tunnelling & Ergonomics — Module 3 (p. 91–100)", "excerpt": "Slide 91 Slide 92 Slide 93 Slide 94 Slide 95 Slide 96 Slide 97 Slide 98 Slide 99 Slide 100", "section": "Slide 91", "version": "1:76ab940581b91568", "sourceId": "3d013096-89b2-42cb-b638-88e6f3c75362", "sourceType": "document"}, {"page": 101, "label": "Construction Safety, Tunnelling & Ergonomics — Module 3 (p. 101–110)", "excerpt": "Slide 101 Slide 102 Slide 103 Slide 104 Slide 105 Slide 106 Slide 107 Slide 108 Slide 109 Musculoskeletal Disorder Musculoskeletal Disorders or MSDs are injuries and disorders that affect the human body’s movement or musculoskeletal system (i.e. muscles, tendons, ligaments, nerves, discs, blood vessels, etc.). Common musculoskeletal disorders include: Carpal Tunnel Syndrome : A numbness and tingling in the hand and arm caused by a pinched nerve in the wrist. Tendonitis : inflammation or irritation of a tendon Muscle / Tendon strain Ligament Sprain Tension Neck Syndrome Other common names for MSDs are “repetitive motion injury”, “repetitive stress injury”, “overuse injury” and many more. Slide 110 Thoracic Outlet Compression : a group of disorders that occur when blood vessels or nerves in the space between your collarbone and your first rib (thoracic outlet) are compressed Rotator Cuff Tendonitis Epicondylitis Radial Tunnel Syndrome Digital Neuritis Trigger Finger / Thumb DeQuervain’s Syndrome Mechanical Back Syndrome Degenerative Disc Disease Ruptured / Herniated Disc, and many more.", "section": "Slide 101", "version": "1:76ab940581b91568", "sourceId": "3d013096-89b2-42cb-b638-88e6f3c75362", "sourceType": "document"}, {"page": 111, "label": "Construction Safety, Tunnelling & Ergonomics — Module 3 (p. 111–115)", "excerpt": "Slide 111 The Cause of Musculoskeletal Disorders – Exposure to Risk Factors These risk factors can be broken up into two categories: work-related (ergonomic) risk factors individual-related risk factors Slide 112 Slide 113 Work-related Risk Factors There are three primary ergonomic risk factors. High task repetition. Many work tasks and cycles are repetitive in nature, and are frequently controlled by hourly or daily production targets and work processes. High task repetition, when combined with other risks factors such high force and/or awkward postures, can contribute to the formation of MSD. A job is considered highly repetitive if the cycle time is 30 seconds or less. Forceful exertions. Many work tasks require high force loads on the human body. Muscle effort increases in response to high force requirements, increasing associated fatigue which can lead to MSD. Repetitive or sustained awkward postures. Awkward postures place excessive force on joints and overload the muscles and tendons around the effected joint. Joints of the body are most efficient when they operate closest to the mid-range motion of the joint. Risk of MSD is increased when joints are worked outside of this m", "section": "Slide 111", "version": "1:76ab940581b91568", "sourceId": "3d013096-89b2-42cb-b638-88e6f3c75362", "sourceType": "document"}], "createdAt": "2026-09-02T12:10:25.683Z", "expiresAt": "2026-09-02T12:40:25.677Z", "studentId": "0cd6f10d-fa83-4df4-a3b4-5d9d202aace5", "sessionType": "learn", "accessibility": [], "correlationId": "ec985662-cf68-46d3-b7d2-822254c44b3c", "institutionId": "dc099dcb-289c-476b-99ed-d29d486b7c7b", "schemaVersion": "petros.session-intent.v1", "idempotencyKey": "d33b0f74-be85-4e8b-a340-f0e9fe97ca2c", "targetConcepts": ["MODULE 3", "SUMMARY", "SUMMARY", "Slide 31", "Slide 41", "TUNNELLING", "GENERAL SAFETY PRECAUTIONS IN TUNNELLING", "SAFETY PRECAUTIONS", "Slide 81", "Slide 91", "Slide 101", "Slide 111"], "homeworkContext": null, "registerFactors": ["The student explicitly asked to be taught from their own uploaded pages.", "Keep source grounding visible and separate explanation from quotation."], "sourceEntityIds": ["3d013096-89b2-42cb-b638-88e6f3c75362", "bd160c21-ca9f-44e4-93f7-be374370a8ca", "15f162e7-fa9c-4eec-900f-8b92cbbfbeee", "0f7318e9-c315-4305-8a89-b9a0592ad40c", "3f147bc2-0983-4ccf-b1bc-b975a3eca682", "1fb7e4c5-2d9f-4245-86db-7b8679595e40", "6ec51f82-5ece-4f90-ba8c-65d4d4502c90", "a4deb8c7-5e04-4945-b2ed-43e2d074e25f", "03f0f4e7-cd6b-4524-8e7e-868166a184a4", "e813e1ca-a989-4c78-a054-bd15e0b93531", "91232e08-6ca8-43e5-b251-65ad93be40ae", "2f6f62f6-c501-49ec-9446-46a3d8fcd933"], "emotionalRegister": "curious_grounded", "permissionContext": ["student.learn.use", "student.library.use", "student.lab.use", "student.history.view", "student.achievements.view", "student.analytics.view", "student.calendar.view", "student.planner.manage", "student.homework.vi